WEBVTT

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.150
Someone literally came
to me and was like,

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.150
hey, have you ever
thought about advertising,

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.334
like your NPR? When I was at
school, I was doing fashion shows.

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:08.774
I was just doing all
the things, right?

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:10.694
Cause I was interested in
fashion and entrepreneurship.

00:00:10.694 --> 00:00:14.054
And so I was doing stuff on
campus that somebody literally was

00:00:14.054 --> 00:00:16.046
like, you should think
about advertising.

00:00:16.102 --> 00:00:18.662
And so at the time, I hadn't
heard anything about it,

00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:20.822
just like entrepreneurship
when I was in high school.

00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:23.542
And I was like, this might be
another one of those moments.

00:00:23.542 --> 00:00:25.222
So I went all the way into it.

00:00:25.222 --> 00:00:26.822
I went and got books
on advertising.

00:00:26.822 --> 00:00:28.206
I went to the library.

00:00:28.262 --> 00:00:32.262
And I started to shift from like
PR to like I was going into more.

00:00:32.262 --> 00:00:33.782
I wasn't really sure
what I wanted to do.

00:00:33.782 --> 00:00:35.566
And I was like, this might be it.

00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:37.686
And so I went hard there.

00:00:37.686 --> 00:00:40.646
And that same person that was like,
you should think about advertising

00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:42.510
introduced me to an internship.

00:00:42.678 --> 00:00:45.398
I applied for the internship
with the Marcus Graham Project.

00:00:45.398 --> 00:00:47.318
And it's going to be, it's
going to be a lot of people.

00:00:47.318 --> 00:00:49.518
When you find people
like Britney, like,

00:00:49.606 --> 00:00:52.390
Follow them. Listen to them and
follow them because it literally

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.950
started for me in the same
way in a class like this.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.910
I found another person just like
her who was Lincoln Stephens,

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.190
who did the Marcus Graham Project,
which was an incubator for

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.030
pulling kids into advertising.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.558
So, mind you. I
come from Roseland.

00:01:05.558 --> 00:01:06.958
I went to school here.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.470
My education is extremely important,
but it's all the things that

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.750
I did outside of my education
that got me to where I'm at.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.438
So the entrepreneurship class.

00:01:15.462 --> 00:01:17.920
the advertising internship
that I had to apply for.
